## 1. Poisson Distribution Formula
\\[ \boxed{P(X=x)=\frac{e^{-\lambda}\lambda^x}{x!}} \\]
Where:
- \\(\lambda\\) = Mean number of occurrences
- \\(x\\) = Number of occurrences
- \\(e \approx 2.71828\\)
- \\(x!\\) = Factorial of \\(x\\)

## 2. Normal Distribution Formula
\\[ \boxed{f(x)=\frac{1}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-\frac{(x-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}}} \\]
Where:
- \\(\mu\\) = Mean
- \\(\sigma\\) = Standard deviation
- \\(\sigma^2\\) = Variance
- \\(x\\) = Observed value
Remember: Poisson is used to calculate the probability of a number of events occurring in a fixed interval, while normal distribution describes continuous data following a bell-shaped curve.


